course: chemistry
lesson: 5
title: Melting, freezing, boiling and condensing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Water can be ice, liquid water or invisible water vapour, and it moves between these states when it is heated or cooled.
explain: Heat makes particles shake harder until they break free: ice melts, and water evaporates or boils; cooling slows them so they stick together again.
explain: On a hot day an ice lolly drips because the warm air gives the ice heat; a cold can "sweats" because water vapour from the air turns back into drops on the cold metal.
explain: The words are melting (solid to liquid), freezing (liquid to solid), evaporating or boiling (liquid to gas) and condensing (gas to liquid).
explain: Water is strange: it grows bigger when it freezes, which is why ice floats and a full bottle cracks in the freezer.
fact: Ice melts at 0 °C; chocolate melts at about 34 °C, just below the temperature inside your mouth.
fact: Evaporation happens at the surface of a liquid at any temperature, when the fastest particles escape as vapour.
fact: Puddles dry faster on sunny, windy days: the sun gives energy and the wind carries the vapour away.
fact: Condensation is water vapour turning back into liquid on a cold surface, which is why a cold can looks wet outside.
fact: You see your breath on a frosty morning because the warm vapour in it condenses into tiny droplets in the cold air.
fact: At sea level water boils at 100 °C; high on a mountain it boils at a lower temperature, so pasta takes longer to cook.
fact: The big bubbles at the bottom of a pan of boiling water are pockets of water that has turned into gas.
fact: Water expands when it freezes, so ice floats, ponds freeze from the top down and frozen pipes can burst.
fact: Frost forms when water vapour turns straight into ice on a surface below freezing.
fact: Dry ice is frozen carbon dioxide that turns straight into gas; it must never be touched with bare hands, because it burns with cold.
fact: Salt makes ice melt below 0 °C, because salty water freezes at a lower temperature; it stops working well in very hard frost.
fact: Water evaporating from your skin takes heat away, which is why sweat cools you and wet hair feels cold.
fact: Ice and salt together get much colder than ice alone, cold enough to freeze ice cream in a bag.
exercise: Why does an ice lolly drip on a hot day? | Heat from the warm air melts the ice | The lolly sweats water from inside | Sunlight turns the ice into juice gas | The stick soaks up and lets out water
exercise: Where does the water from a puddle go? | It evaporates into the air as water vapour | It all soaks deep down into the ground, every last drop of it | The sun burns it up completely | Birds and animals drink all of it
exercise: A cold can of drink is wet outside. Where did the water come from? | Water vapour in the air condensed on the cold can | The drink inside leaked out through tiny holes in the metal | The can was washed before it came out | The cold metal makes its own water
exercise: Why can you see your breath on a frosty morning? | The vapour in your breath condenses into tiny drops in the cold air | Cold air turns your warm breath into a thin grey smoke, like a fire does | Your lungs make more water in winter | Frost from the air freezes on your breath
exercise: What are the bubbles at the bottom of a pan of boiling water? | Pockets of water that have turned into gas | Air that was hiding in the pan | Tiny bits of the pan's metal melting | Salt coming out of the water as it heats
exercise: Why did a full bottle of water crack in the freezer? | Water grows bigger when it freezes | Cold makes plastic shrink and split | The ice became heavier than the bottle | Air in the freezer pushed on the bottle
exercise: How does frost make feathery patterns on a car windscreen? | Water vapour turns straight into ice crystals on the cold glass | Rain freezes into feathers as soon as it lands on the cold glass of the car | Snowflakes stick to the glass and melt | The glass grows ice from inside
exercise: Why do people sprinkle salt on an icy pavement? | Salt makes the ice melt even below 0 °C | Salt makes the ice warmer than 20 °C | Salt soaks up the ice like a sponge | Salt makes the pavement rough to walk on
exercise: Why does wet hair feel cold when you come out of the pool? | Evaporating water takes heat away from you | Pool water is always ice cold, even when it is heated | Chlorine in the water freezes your hair | Wet hair lets the wind into your head
exercise: Why can ice and salt in a bag freeze ice cream? | Salted ice gets much colder than ordinary ice | Salt is a very cold substance all on its own | The bag traps cold air from the fridge | Shaking the bag hard makes it cold
exercise: Why does chocolate melt in your mouth but not in the cupboard? | It melts just below the temperature inside your mouth | Your spit dissolves it, just like sugar dissolves in a cup of hot tea | Cupboards keep chocolate frozen solid | Your teeth crush it into a liquid
exercise: What is the word for a gas turning into a liquid? | Condensing | Melting | Evaporating | Freezing
exercise: Why is it harder to cook pasta at the top of a mountain? | Water boils at a lower temperature up there | The air is far too thin for a flame to burn | Pasta goes hard at high places | Water never boils on mountains
exercise: Why do puddles dry faster on windy, sunny days? | The sun gives energy and the wind carries the vapour away | The wind blows the puddle water away down the street and into the drains | Sunlight makes the water sink into the soil | Wind makes the puddle colder
test: Your friend says: "That can of cola is leaking, look, it's all wet outside." How could you show the water came from the air? | Put a cold empty can or glass out and it gets wet too | Weigh the can every hour and see that it is slowly getting lighter | Taste the water to see if it is cola | Shake the can to see if it leaks more
test: What would happen to a pond in winter if ice sank instead of floating? | It could freeze from the bottom up, and fish would be in danger | Fish would be much warmer in winter, under a thick blanket of ice | Nothing, ponds never freeze | The ice would melt at once
test: "it's minus 25 out, why aren't they salting the roads, lazy council" What is a better reason? | Salt does not melt ice well in such hard frost | Salt only works on roads in the summer months | Salt makes ice thicker at minus 25 | Roads cannot get icy below minus 20
test: A cartoon shows someone picking up dry ice with bare hands to make "party smoke". What is wrong? | Dry ice burns skin with cold and must never be touched bare | Nothing, dry ice is just as safe to hold as the ice cubes from the freezer | Dry ice melts into water on hands | Dry ice is hot and burns like fire
test: Your little brother says: "Puddles go away because the ground drinks them all." Put him right. | Much of the water evaporates into the air | He is right, the air takes none of it at all | The water turns into mud forever | Clouds suck puddles up through straws
test: "why'm I freezing getting out the pool when it's windy, bloody hell" Best answer? | The wind speeds up evaporation, which takes heat from you | The wind is always colder than the water | Wind blows the cold pool water back all over you, again and again | Your skin stops working in wind
test: Why can you see your breath in winter but not in summer? | In warm air the vapour stays an invisible gas | You breathe out much less air in summer | Summer breath has no water in it | Summer sunlight hides your breath
test: Your cousin says: "Boiling means the water is 100 degrees, so it's the same on every mountain." Put him right. | Water boils at a lower temperature high up | He is right, it is always 100 degrees everywhere | Water boils hotter on mountains | Water can't boil above the clouds
test: A bag of ice and salt feels too cold to hold. Why? | The salted ice melts and draws heat from everything around, your hands too | The salt in the bag is frozen solid, and frozen salt is the coldest thing there is | Salt bites the skin like a crab | The bag is made of cold plastic
test: Why do frozen water pipes sometimes burst in winter? | Water expands as it freezes and pushes the pipe apart | Ice is very sharp and cuts the pipe open from inside | Cold metal turns to powder | Frozen water gets heavier and drops
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises condensation? | The conversion of a vapour into a liquid upon contact with a cooler surface | The conversion of a solid into a liquid by the application of heat from without | The conversion of a liquid into a vapour at its surface | The conversion of a liquid into a solid by cooling
exam: It is given that a sealed vessel completely filled with water has been placed in a freezer and is subsequently found fractured. Identify the principal cause. | The water expanded in volume upon freezing | The water contracted upon freezing and drew the walls inward | The vessel was attacked by the freezer's cold air | The ice acquired a greater mass than the water
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the boiling of water at high altitude. | Water boils at a temperature lower than 100 °C | Water boils at a temperature higher than 100 °C | Water cannot be brought to the boil | Water boils at exactly 100 °C invariably
exam: Identify the principal reason for which evaporation of water from the skin produces a sensation of cold. | The escaping particles carry heat away from the body | Water is invariably colder than the skin | Evaporation introduces cold air into the skin | The skin produces cold in response to moisture
